  "content": {
    "id": "p50-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "Show that for all real $m$, the equation $x^2-(m^2+m)x+m^3=0$ has real roots, possibly equal, with one root equal to the square of the other.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\Delta=m^2(m-1)^2\\geq0$. The roots are $m$ and $m^2$, so one is the square of the other. They coincide when $m=0$ or $m=1$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\Delta&=\\bigl(-(m^2+m)\\bigr)^2-4(1)(m^3)\\\\\\Delta&=m^4+2m^3+m^2-4m^3\\\\\\Delta&=m^4-2m^3+m^2\\\\\\Delta&=m^2(m-1)^2\\geq0\\end{aligned}$$\nThe roots are real for every real $m$. Since $\\sqrt{\\Delta}=|m(m-1)|$, the two signs in the quadratic formula give the same pair of values as $\\pm m(m-1)$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}x_1&=\\frac{m^2+m+m(m-1)}{2}=m^2\\\\x_2&=\\frac{m^2+m-m(m-1)}{2}=m\\end{aligned}$$\nThus $x_1=x_2^2$. The roots coincide when $m^2=m$, namely $m=0$ or $m=1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 85
  },
